A1. There are many books on this in any large library (plur.). ~ SHELVES
A7. An absinthe-minded fellow. ~ SOT
A10. An estate. ~ DOMAIN
A13. The son of Abe. ~ IKE
A14. A jocose fellow from the jungle. ~ APE
A15. You will have to go to law about this. ~ EDICT
A16. This is keeping under cover these days. ~ KNEE
A17. The piper's son. ~ TOM
A18. The beer that made Milwaukee famous. ~ BOCK
A19. Just desserts. ~ PIES
A20. This is apt to make any sinner thinner. ~ LISP
A21. Beware when on the road—this might be catching. ~ ARRESTING
A23. Get out your old school grammar for this. ~ PARSE
A24. You might use force for this one. ~ VIS
A25. This is how Lake Huron ends and Ontario begins. ~ ON
A26. This may not be professional, but we put it in just for sport. ~ AMATEUR
A28. A bone from the tail of the rhinoceros. ~ OS
A29. A good thing to take when you go to the big city. ~ CARE
A31. What the old-fashioned grandmother is. ~ RARE
A32. The way mother looks in pajamas. ~ MA
A33. The way a good gardener starts asparagus. ~ ASP
A34. Just a little bit of nonsense. ~ NON
A35. You will find this at the extreme end of the suburb. ~ RB
A37. A portico. ~ STOA
A39. A deposit on a bank. ~ SILT
A40. This sounds modern but it is all Greek to us. ~ NU
A41. Speeders. ~ DASHERS
A43. The way father looks in pajamas. ~ PA
A44. This came down from the North woods last winter and got all lit up. ~ FIR
A45. This one gets sore unless it is all dressed up. ~ ULCER
A46. One who can give a lot of pointers on fur. ~ PORCUPINE
A50. Those who do this never make 60 miles an hour but they might make 60 cents an hour. ~ PLOD
A51. What Charles Augustus Lindbergh Junior is. ~ TINY
A52. A dry spell. ~ SERE
A53. This is a meal in itself. ~ OAT
A54. The kind of calves that are seen running around the beaches. ~ BARE
A55. What hubby doesn't dare bring home to eat. ~ GUEST
A56. The way the tourist looks after the tour. ~ IST
A57. Right here we are going to put in a word for Yale. ~ ELI
A58. The kind of applause Will Rogers gets. ~ HEARTY
A59. The country home that is in style now. ~ STY
A60. We had to put this in just to be polite. ~ GENTEEL

D1. In Chicago they call these gunny. ~ SACKS
D2. When mother took up club work this was an awful blow to father. ~ HIT
D3. This might be inoffensive. (Ask the printer.) ~ EN
D4. Mother makes this one work. ~ VINEGAR
D5. What a housewife does when she has a hard time to budget. ~ EKES
D6. Any good-looker can do this. ~ SEE
D7. This is some trenchant wit. ~ SATIRE
D8. Don't you ever think this is a dead one. ~ OPOSSUM
D9. When this begins to go up, you had better get in with the shorts and be a bare. ~ TEMPERATURE
D10. This is all rubbish. ~ DEBRIS
D11. Whether good or bad you are on the right scent (plur.). ~ ODORS
D12. This is what makes the holes in Swiss cheese (plur.). ~ MICE
D16. Domestic and foreign relations. ~ KIN
D19. A native of Georgia. ~ PINE
D20. There is a hidden meaning in this. ~ LATENT
D21. This often puts up a good front. ~ AVOIRDUPOIS
D22. This lacks action. ~ TORPOR
D23. Oral (law). ~ PAROL
D27. This one has an ultra-violent way. ~ MANIAC
D29. This might be turned into money. ~ CASHED
D30. This blooming thing comes in September. ~ ASTER
D36. This will keep you steady. ~ BALLAST
D38. What the modern Cleopatra would take. ~ ASPIRIN
D39. This describes the only living pedestrians. ~ SPRY
D40. The degrees they get at summer schools. ~ NINETY
D42. A very close friend. ~ SCOTTY
D44. This is always starting something. ~ FIRST
D47. Put a cap on this and it will look like Capone. ~ ONE
D48. Don't kick—this has always been customary in crossword puzzles. ~ USUAL
D49. What a tiger might do through the bars of a cage. ~ PEER
D51. This is our story and we are going to stick to it. ~ TALE
D54. This often precedes a pardon. ~ BEG
D55. Keep to the right. (For an ox-ford.) ~ GEE
D58. The man that started and ended her heartache. ~ HE


